Make GDAL's own Python bindings buildable without a runnable `gdal-config`,
by letting the environment answer every question setup.py would have asked
it, and by letting the recipe choose the libraries to link.

The bindings' setup.py resolves the native library through
`gdal_ext.get_gdal_config(option)`, which calls `fetch_config()` — a
`subprocess.Popen(["gdal-config", "--<option>"])`. Three options are asked
for: `version` (to check that libgdal is at least as new as the bindings),
`prefix` (whose `lib/` and `include/` are appended to the link and compile
paths when nothing else supplied them) and `cflags`. A fourth global,
`libraries = ['gdal']`, is fixed in the module body.

None of that survives a cross build. `recipes/flet-libgdal/build.sh` ends
with `rm -rf $PREFIX/{bin,share}`, so the flet-libgdal wheel ships headers
and libraries under `{platlib}/opt` and no `gdal-config`; `fetch_config`
raises `gdal_config_error`, and `finalize_options` turns that into
"Could not find gdal-config. Make sure you have installed the GDAL native
library and development headers." (The `version` call is wrapped in its own
`except gdal_config_error: pass`, so a missing config tool silently skips
the bindings-vs-library version guard as well.) Should the build host happen
to carry a Homebrew or system `gdal-config`, the call succeeds instead and
hands the cross compile the host's macOS prefix — worse, because it builds
green.

Hunk 2 short-circuits `get_gdal_config`: an option is looked up as
`GDAL_<OPTION>` in the environment first, and only falls through to the
subprocess when that variable is absent. The naming is mechanical, so the
recipe's `script_env` answers all three calls — `GDAL_VERSION: 3.13.1`,
`GDAL_PREFIX: {platlib}/opt`, `GDAL_CFLAGS: ''`. The empty `GDAL_CFLAGS` is
deliberate rather than an oversight: it is present (so no subprocess runs)
but falsy (so `if cflags:` adds nothing), which is exactly "no extra compile
flags, and don't go looking".

Hunk 1 lets `GDAL_LIBS` replace the hard-wired `libraries = ['gdal']`
(comma-separated — library names, not paths). On Android the default is
right and `meta.yaml` leaves it alone. On iOS every native dep is a static
archive and `libgdal.a` was archived with the symbols it references but does
not define — `_geod_init` (libproj), `_TIFFClientOpen` (libtiff),
`_psl_builtin` (libpsl) — left undefined; the SWIG `_gdal.so` inherits those
references, `-undefined dynamic_lookup` defers them to dlopen, and iOS dyld
resolves the flat namespace eagerly and aborts at first import. Listing the
whole chain makes the extension link pull those objects out of the archives.
`meta.yaml` carries the full explanation and the pointer to the proper fix
in `flet-libgdal`'s iOS cmake invocation.

Two things to know when this patch stops applying. The `libraries` hunk's
context contains absolute paths from *upstream's* release machine
(`/home/runner/work/gdal/...`) — that text is GDAL's own, baked into the
sdist by their build, and it changes between releases, so the context has to
be refreshed at every version bump (it already moved once, from
`/home/even/gdal/3.10/...`). And this recipe is pinned to the exact
`flet-libgdal` version, so a bump here is never a lone edit.

Rationale: hunk 1 was added in PR #65 ("Support testing recipes on Android
emulator and iOS simulator and fixes issues found"), which documents the
dyld cascade; PR #68 rebased both hunks onto GDAL 3.13.1's setup.py. Hunk 2
predates both, arriving in the bulk consolidation commit ec8d184 with no
recorded reason — its account above is read off the diff, GDAL 3.13.1's
setup.py, and the `GDAL_VERSION`/`GDAL_PREFIX`/`GDAL_CFLAGS` trio in this
recipe's `script_env`, which exists only to feed it.

diff --git a/setup.py b/setup.py
--- a/setup.py
+++ b/setup.py
@@ -55,6 +55,8 @@
     include_dirs = ['/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/python_pkgs/port', '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/port', '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/python_pkgs/gcore', '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/gcore',  '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/gcore/multidim', '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/alg', '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/ogr/', '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/ogr/ogrsf_frmts', '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/gnm', '/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/apps']
     library_dirs = ['/home/runner/work/gdal/gdal/python_pkgs']
 libraries = ['gdal']
+if 'GDAL_LIBS' in os.environ:
+    libraries = os.environ['GDAL_LIBS'].split(',')


 # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ -240,6 +242,9 @@

     def get_gdal_config(self, option):
         try:
+            var_name = f"GDAL_{option.upper()}"
+            if var_name in os.environ:
+                return os.environ[var_name]
             return fetch_config(option, gdal_config=self.gdal_config)
         except gdal_config_error:
             msg = 'Could not find gdal-config. Make sure you have installed the GDAL native library and development headers.'
